- op: discuss  # 22d1
  at: '2026-09-11T10:14:19Z'  # 22d1
  by: agent  # 22d1
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 22d1
  clock: 4  # 22d1
  nonce: 22d1  # 22d1
  message: "DONE — arc T10, PR #916. Record: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/jozap_09112026/\n(PLAN.md pre-registered before any arm, two dated addenda, arm.sh,\ndiff_seeds.py, adjudicate_direct.py, RESULT.md).\n\nTHE CONTROL RAN BEFORE THE FIX AND IT FIRED. Pre-fix tree, `elixir.py` blob\n`8fe54c20cb…` asserted identical in both arms, phoenix-framework, fresh\ninterpreters:\n\n    PYTHONHASHSEED=0      survey 17,157,886 bytes\n    PYTHONHASHSEED=12345  survey 17,171,522 bytes\n    elixir edges 8,049 both   APPEARED 101   VANISHED 101\n\nAND THE CHURN'S CONTENT INDICTS THE ATTRIBUTION, NOT ONLY THE ORDERING: the\nmovers are `raise`, `to_string`, `is_list` — Kernel auto-imports, available\nwith no `import` directive at all — flipping between `Plug.Conn` and\n`Phoenix.Controller` by hash seed. `sorted(...)` would have pinned WHICH of two\nwrong answers is given.\n\nTHE ITEM NAMED ONE DEFECT DIMENSION AND THERE WERE TWO. The filing says \"Edge\nTOTALS are stable …; it is the DESTINATIONS that churn\". The pre-fix control\nshows **16/25 NODES differing between seeds as well** — the synthetic stand-in\nsymbols minted for whichever arbitrary module won that run.\n\nTHE FIX. `_ELIXIR_STDLIB_FUNCTIONS` was TWO populations wearing one name:\nKernel auto-imports, and names that genuinely come from an imported module\n(`IO.puts`, `Enum.map`, `String.split`, `Logger.error`), whose ownership was\nwritten down beside them as prose comments and never used as data. Three\nordered rules, no set iteration anywhere: an `only:` list that NAMES the\nfunction wins; a Kernel auto-import gets NO module-keyed edge; an importable\nname whose owning module is imported takes that module; anything else is\ndeclined. The union `_ELIXIR_STDLIB_FUNCTIONS` is membership-IDENTICAL before\nand after (97 = 65 + 32, asserted), so every other branch that gates on it is\nuntouched.\n\nRESULTS.\n\n    P1 determinism (per-run uuid excluded)      edges            nodes\n      BASE seed 0 vs 12345                   101 / 101        16 / 25\n      SUBJ seed 0 vs 12345                     0 / 0            0 / 0\n\n    P2 / P4, read at the ANALYZER            branch edges   lost   REAL losses\n      phoenix-framework                       427 -> 0       427        0\n      livebook                                226 -> 0       226        0\n\n**P1, P2 and P4 all hold. 653 edges removed, 0 real losses**, every one a\nKernel auto-import: `Plug.Conn.inspect` (43), `Plug.Conn.raise` (36),\n`Plug.Conn.is_atom` (21), `Ecto.Changeset.raise` (15), `Record.is_binary` (11),\n`Livebook.Factory.send` (7).\n\nTHE FINDING THIS DID NOT EXPECT, and it is not buried: **on both repositories\nthe branch emitted 653 edges and every single one was false.** It never once\nfired on the population WI-rakul wrote it for, so on this corpus the change is\nPURELY SUBTRACTIVE.\n\nWHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED: rules 1 and 3 — the two that PRESERVE an attribution —\nhave NO live witness. Neither fired on either repository; they are pinned only\nby the twelve unit tests. A rule that preserves and has no corpus witness could\nbe wrong in a way this measurement cannot see. Two Elixir repositories is also\nnot a population.\n\nTHREE INSTRUMENT FAULTS CAUGHT, each by a number that could not be true. (1) A\nfirst P1 reading `md5sum`-ed a survey file that was STILL BEING WRITTEN and\nreported a difference — a partial file and a different file look alike to\n`md5sum`. (2) A first P2/P3 instrument keyed the dst on `:unresolved`, which is\nre-keyed to `:external_symbol` downstream, and returned a clean **BASE=0**.\n(3) A second keyed on the dst pattern alone and reported **2,111 \"unsupported\"\nsurviving edges** that were `Path.join` and `Enum.map` — qualified calls\nneeding no import. The population is now read at the analyzer, where\n`meta['binding']` survives; the survey drops it.\n\nTHE SHAPE COUNT WAS FIVE AND IT IS SIX. The pre-registration recorded five\n`import` shapes from a grammar probe; writing the coverage tests found\n`import :erlang` (an ATOM argument, no `alias` node) and a second-keyword\nspelling (`only: [...], warn: false`) the reader must walk past. Enumerating\nwhat a grammar CAN produce is not enumerating what it DOES — and the coverage\ngate, not the probe, is what surfaced the difference."  # 22d1
